<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p>Studying the acoustics of enclosed spaces is important for
  improving the quality of environments and reducing noise-induced
  health effects. With the rise of numerical room acoustics modelling,
  tools are needed to predict sound behaviour in enclosed spaces.
  <monospace>acousticDE</monospace> is open-source software for
  simulating room acoustics using the diffusion equation approach. The
  software is suitable for research applications, consultancy work, and
  educational purposes. In practice, it can be used for evaluating
  design choices during early architectural design stages, optimising
  acoustic treatment layouts, or testing the performance of rooms for
  speech, music, or general occupancy.</p>
  <p>The method implemented in <monospace>acousticDE</monospace>—the
  diffusion equation (DE) method—offers a balance between computational
  efficiency and flexibility across room types. To simulate a room sound
  field, <monospace>acousticDE</monospace> requires a digital 3D model
  of the geometry and the material properties describing the acoustic
  boundaries. Once configured, <monospace>acousticDE</monospace> can
  incorporate sound sources, compute the sound energy throughout the
  space, and provide key room acoustics parameters for acoustic
  assessments.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>Room acoustics simulation is essential for researchers and
  practitioners to accurately predict and design the acoustics inside a
  room. However, existing tools often face a trade-off between (1)
  accuracy and efficiency in the calculation method and (2) transparency
  in the software’s functionality and operation. Traditional statistical
  methods, such as the Sabine and Eyring calculations, provide a fast
  global estimate of the acoustic parameters, but these are based on
  unrealistic assumptions that are only valid for certain rooms and
  within certain frequency ranges
  (<xref alt="Kuttruff, 2009" rid="ref-Kuttruff2019RoomAcoustics" ref-type="bibr">Kuttruff,
  2009</xref>). More accurate approaches are available, including both
  commercial and open-source software. However, commercial tools often
  lack transparency, as it is difficult to understand how the
  calculations are performed internally. Available tools are mostly
  based on common approaches for running high-frequency simulations,
  i.e., geometrical acoustics methods. Additionally, full wave-based
  approaches can achieve higher accuracy but require significant
  computational resources, making them impractical for larger
  spaces.</p>
  <p><monospace>acousticDE</monospace> was developed to address both
  these gaps by providing a transparent and efficient method for
  high-frequency room acoustics.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the field</title>
  <p>The DE model is a compromise between accuracy and efficiency for
  room acoustics simulations in the high-frequency range
  (<xref alt="Foy et al., 2016" rid="ref-Foy2016IncludingApproach" ref-type="bibr">Foy
  et al., 2016</xref>;
  <xref alt="Mou et al., 2023" rid="ref-Mou2023AnSpaces" ref-type="bibr">Mou
  et al., 2023</xref>;
  <xref alt="Sü Gül et al., 2020" rid="ref-SuGul2020ComparativeStations" ref-type="bibr">Sü
  Gül et al., 2020</xref>). However, despite its demonstrated usefulness
  in the literature, accessible and user-friendly open-source software
  implementations of the acoustic DE model do not exist
  (<xref alt="Hornikx et al., 2024" rid="ref-Hornikx2024" ref-type="bibr">Hornikx
  et al., 2024</xref>) or are somewhat limited. In fact, most
  implementations of the DE are embedded in commercial tools (e.g.,
  COMSOL or the Femlab toolbox for MATLAB), where room acoustics
  simulations are not the primary focus. An additional open-source
  implementation exists, but it is currently at its initial experimental
  stage, lacks documentation, and is written in the GNU Octave
  programming language
  (<xref alt="Environmental Acoustics Research Unit, University Gustave Eiffel, n.d." rid="ref-univgustaveeiffel" ref-type="bibr">Environmental
  Acoustics Research Unit, University Gustave Eiffel, n.d.</xref>).</p>
  <p>Compared to the available software,
  <monospace>acousticDE</monospace> distinguishes itself by providing
  two different numerical methods for implementing the DE model within a
  Python programming environment: the Du Fort–Frankel finite difference
  method (FDM), suitable for parallelepiped shapes
  (<xref alt="Navarro et al., 2012" rid="ref-Navarro2012ImplementationRooms" ref-type="bibr">Navarro
  et al., 2012</xref>), and the finite volume method (FVM), which
  supports arbitrary 3D geometry
  (<xref alt="Pagán Muñoz, 2019" rid="ref-PaganMunoz2019NumericalMethods" ref-type="bibr">Pagán
  Muñoz, 2019</xref>). The dual approach allows the user to select the
  appropriate implementation depending on the room type and the
  dimension of the discretization. Compared to other tools for the
  high-frequency range, such as pyroomacoustics
  (<xref alt="Scheibler et al., 2018" rid="ref-Scheibler2018" ref-type="bibr">Scheibler
  et al., 2018</xref>) and sound particle tracing
  (<xref alt="Picaut &amp; Fortin, 2012" rid="ref-ISimpa2012" ref-type="bibr">Picaut
  &amp; Fortin, 2012</xref>), the DE model is easy to use and requires
  little computation time. However, for non-proportional geometries or
  highly absorptive spaces, the diffusion equation does not reach the
  level of accuracy of more detailed (but computationally more costly)
  acoustics methods (geometrical acoustics or wave-based methods). It is
  instead accurate for compact spaces with highly reflective
  surfaces.</p>
  <p>In addition to standard room-acoustics parameters,
  <monospace>acousticDE</monospace> can generate impulse responses and
  support auralization, enabling perceptual audio demonstrations of
  simulated acoustic conditions. Comprehensive and well-structured
  documentation accompanies the software.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="method">
  <title>Method</title>
  <p>The DE method, originally introduced by Ollendorff
  (<xref alt="Ollendorff, 1969" rid="ref-Ollendorff1969AbsorptionWaves" ref-type="bibr">Ollendorff,
  1969</xref>) and later refined
  (<xref alt="Picaut et al., 1997" rid="ref-Picaut1997AEquation" ref-type="bibr">Picaut
  et al., 1997</xref>), overcomes some of the limitations of statistical
  room acoustics, which is grounded in the diffuse-field assumption
  (<xref alt="Sabine, 1922" rid="ref-Sabine1922CollectedAcoustics" ref-type="bibr">Sabine,
  1922</xref>). Compared to wave-based approaches
  (<xref alt="Hamilton &amp; Bilbao, 2017" rid="ref-Hamilton2017FDTDTime" ref-type="bibr">Hamilton
  &amp; Bilbao, 2017</xref>;
  <xref alt="Wang et al., 2019" rid="ref-Wang2019RoomMethod" ref-type="bibr">Wang
  et al., 2019</xref>), ray- and beam-tracing algorithms
  (<xref alt="Kuttruff, 2009" rid="ref-Kuttruff2019RoomAcoustics" ref-type="bibr">Kuttruff,
  2009</xref>), or the image source method
  (<xref alt="Kuttruff, 2009" rid="ref-Kuttruff2019RoomAcoustics" ref-type="bibr">Kuttruff,
  2009</xref>), the DE method is generally more accurate than Sabine- or
  Eyring-based estimates while remaining computationally
  inexpensive.</p>
  <p>The DE method estimates how acoustic energy is distributed over
  time and space within a room. It solves the diffusion equation, which
  is applicable in the high-frequency range (above the Schroeder
  frequency) and assumes only diffusely reflecting boundaries following
  Lambert’s law
  (<xref alt="Valeau et al., 2006" rid="ref-Valeau2006OnPrediction" ref-type="bibr">Valeau
  et al., 2006</xref>). This method is based on the energetical
  approach, where sound has undergone multiple reflections and
  scattering in an environment. It represents the late stage of wave
  propagation, where the energy, after being reflected multiple times,
  is spread out across the space, following the diffusion process of gas
  and heat in a medium. Surface absorption, atmospheric absorption, and
  diffusion are included. Limitations include the omission of
  diffraction, early reflections, and partially scattering surfaces, as
  well as reduced accuracy at high average absorption coefficients.</p>
  <p>The governing diffusion equation and boundary condition are:</p>
  <p><disp-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[
  \frac{\partial{w(\mathbf{r}, t)}} {\partial{t}} = D \boldsymbol{\nabla}^2 w(\mathbf{r}, t) - m c w(\mathbf{r}, t) + q(\mathbf{r}, t)
  ]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mfrac><mml:mrow><mml:mi>∂</mml:mi><mml:mrow><mml:mi>w</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐫</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>∂</mml:mi><mml:mi>t</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mfrac><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi>D</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mi>𝛁</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mi>w</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐫</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>−</mml:mo><mml:mi>m</mml:mi><mml:mi>c</mml:mi><mml:mi>w</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐫</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>+</mml:mo><mml:mi>q</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐫</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></p>
  <p><disp-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[
  -D \dfrac{\partial w}{\partial n} = h w
  ]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mi>D</mml:mi><mml:mstyle displaystyle="true"><mml:mfrac><mml:mrow><mml:mi>∂</mml:mi><mml:mi>w</mml:mi></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mi>∂</mml:mi><mml:mi>n</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:mfrac></mml:mstyle><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mi>h</mml:mi><mml:mi>w</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></p>
  <p>where <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[w(\mathbf{r}, t)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>w</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐫</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is the energy density at each position <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\mathbf{r}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>𝐫</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and at time <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[t]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>t</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[m]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>m</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is the atmospheric attenuation coefficient,
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[c]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>c</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is the speed of sound, <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[q(\mathbf{r}, t)]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>q</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐫</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is the source term, and <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[h]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>h</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is the boundary absorption term. Several methodologies propose
  expressions for <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[h]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>h</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  based on different assumptions on how to treat the boundary surface
  absorption: the Sabine
  (<xref alt="Picaut, 2002" rid="ref-Picaut2002NumericalProcess" ref-type="bibr">Picaut,
  2002</xref>), the Eyring
  (<xref alt="Jing &amp; Xiang, 2007" rid="ref-Jing2007APredication" ref-type="bibr">Jing
  &amp; Xiang, 2007</xref>), and the modified absorption term
  (<xref alt="Jing &amp; Xiang, 2008" rid="ref-Jing2008OnExperiments" ref-type="bibr">Jing
  &amp; Xiang, 2008</xref>). The diffusion coefficient
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[D]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>D</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  with units of m<inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[^2]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi></mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>/s,
  indicates how quickly sound diffuses around the room. This depends on
  the room mean free path.</p>
  <p>From the energy density, the reverberant-field sound pressure level
  (SPL) can be calculated as follows:</p>
  <p><disp-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[
  \text{SPL}(\mathbf{r},t) = 10  \log_{10} \left(\frac{\rho c^2 w(\mathbf{r},t)} {p_{\text{ref}}^2}\right)
  ]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">SPL</mml:mtext><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐫</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:msub><mml:mi>log</mml:mi><mml:mo>&#8289;</mml:mo><mml:mn>10</mml:mn></mml:msub><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mfrac><mml:mrow><mml:mi>ρ</mml:mi><mml:msup><mml:mi>c</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mi>w</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>𝐫</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>t</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:msubsup><mml:mi>p</mml:mi><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">ref</mml:mtext><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msubsup></mml:mfrac><mml:mo stretchy="true" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula></p>
  <p>where <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[\rho]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mi>ρ</mml:mi></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is the density of air in kg/m<inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[^3]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msup><mml:mi></mml:mi><mml:mn>3</mml:mn></mml:msup></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  and <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[p_{\text{ref}}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>p</mml:mi><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">ref</mml:mtext></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  is equal to <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[2 \times 10^ {- 5}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mn>2</mml:mn><mml:mo>×</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mn>10</mml:mn><mml:mrow><mml:mi>−</mml:mi><mml:mn>5</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:msup></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  Pa
  (<xref alt="Navarro &amp; Escolano, 2015" rid="ref-Navarro2015" ref-type="bibr">Navarro
  &amp; Escolano, 2015</xref>).</p>
  <p>This method allows the calculation of room acoustics parameters
  (e.g., sound pressure level and reverberation time). Example results
  are shown in the figure below.</p>
  <p><inline-graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="spldecay.png" />
  <inline-graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="t30freq.png" /></p>
  <p>Figure 1. SPL decay (left) and reverberation time
  <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[T_{30}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:msub><mml:mi>T</mml:mi><mml:mn>30</mml:mn></mml:msub></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
  (right) for a room measuring <inline-formula><alternatives>
  <tex-math><![CDATA[3\,\mathrm{m} \times 3\,\mathrm{m} \times 3\,\mathrm{m}]]></tex-math>
  <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mspace width="0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">m</mml:mi><mml:mo>×</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mspace width="0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">m</mml:mi><mml:mo>×</mml:mo><mml:mn>3</mml:mn><mml:mspace width="0.167em"></mml:mspace><mml:mi mathvariant="normal">m</mml:mi></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>,
  with the source at (1.5 m, 1.5 m, 1.5 m) and the receiver at (2.0 m,
  1.5 m, 1.5 m). The absorption coefficients at the respective
  frequencies are [0.3, 0.33, 0.5, 0.53, 0.7]. This example uses the
  modified absorption term
  (<xref alt="Jing &amp; Xiang, 2008" rid="ref-Jing2008OnExperiments" ref-type="bibr">Jing
  &amp; Xiang, 2008</xref>).</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <sec id="design-trade-offs">
    <title>Design trade-offs</title>
    <p>The design of <monospace>acousticDE</monospace> is shaped by the
    needs of room acoustics simulation research, which are transparency,
    reproducibility, and modularity. The software is implemented in
    Python since it is open-source and widely accessible compared to
    licensed alternatives, such as MATLAB. In addition, it is an easier
    and more understandable language for researchers and users than
    lower-level languages such as C++ and Fortran.</p>
    <p><bold>Method-centric modularity:</bold> Rather than implementing
    a single abstract solver interface,
    <monospace>acousticDE</monospace> is organised around self-contained
    modules: FDM, FVM, and Auralization.</p>
    <p>Each module exposes a clear functional API, such as
    <monospace>run_fvm_sim(...)</monospace>, and maintains its own
    workflow. This structure mirrors typical room-acoustics workflows
    and prioritises clarity over complex abstraction layers. While the
    software is developed as a standalone project, its architecture
    allows for potential future integration or coupling with other tools
    (such as the image source method for the early part of the decay),
    provided that common inputs are used.</p>
    <p><bold>Workflow design:</bold> <monospace>acousticDE</monospace>
    adopts a functional, sequential pipeline rather than an
    object-oriented architecture. The FVM and Auralization pipelines are
    shown below as examples, as they represent the most structurally
    demanding workflows.</p>
    <list list-type="order">
      <list-item>
        <p>Create the 3D geometry.</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p>Mesh the geometry.</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p>Specify the input parameters.</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p>Execute the acoustic diffusion equation simulator.</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p>Execute the auralization module using the results of step
        4.</p>
      </list-item>
    </list>
    <p>This pipeline was intentionally chosen to make each stage of the
    modelling process explicit and inspectable, encouraging users to
    verify the geometry, inspect the mesh, and validate the physical
    inputs before committing to the simulation. This also allows the
    user to create the room geometry using other GUI-based software
    specifically developed for 3D modelling, such as SketchUp or
    Blender, instead of creating the geometry by coding. This design
    makes the modelling process explicit rather than hiding it behind a
    ‘black box’.</p>
    <p><bold>Functional decomposition:</bold> Simulations are executed
    via a single function call. Each module’s main simulation engine
    (step 4 of the pipeline above) is organised into a set of modular,
    sequential function calls. Each function has its own task and
    returns data that are needed by the next function in the next stage
    of the simulation. This functional decomposition improves
    readability and supports maintainability, and it also allows for
    easier adaptation of different steps.</p>
    <p><bold>Array-based data structures:</bold> The core data
    structures used throughout the software are matrices and vectors
    stored as arrays. Arrays are stored in dense and sparse formats
    using the NumPy and SciPy libraries, respectively
    (<xref alt="Harris et al., 2020" rid="ref-harris2020array" ref-type="bibr">Harris
    et al., 2020</xref>;
    <xref alt="Virtanen et al., 2020" rid="ref-2020SciPy-NMeth" ref-type="bibr">Virtanen
    et al., 2020</xref>). This choice reflects the mathematical and
    numerical formulation of the diffusion equation, where discretised
    fields and time-stepping schemes are naturally represented by
    matrices. Mesh information imported from Gmsh
    (<xref alt="Geuzaine &amp; Remacle, 2009" rid="ref-geuzaine-hal-03406365" ref-type="bibr">Geuzaine
    &amp; Remacle, 2009</xref>) is also stored as arrays, maintaining
    consistency. In some cases, Python dictionaries are used for
    convenience (e.g., grouping material properties) but do not affect
    the computational efficiency of the software, since the bulk of the
    computation is performed using NumPy arrays that support
    vectorization and multithreading.</p>
  </sec>
  <sec id="software-validation">
    <title>Software validation</title>
    <p>Results from the software have been validated against published
    studies, measurement results, and external methods such as the
    acoustic radiosity method
    (<xref alt="Koutsouris et al., 2013" rid="ref-Koutsouris2013CombinationMethod" ref-type="bibr">Koutsouris
    et al., 2013</xref>) and the sound particle tracing method
    (<xref alt="Picaut &amp; Fortin, 2012" rid="ref-ISimpa2012" ref-type="bibr">Picaut
    &amp; Fortin, 2012</xref>) and via informal auralization
    experiments. Integration tests of the main simulation engine (step 4
    of the pipeline above) are in place to verify that the complete
    system functions seamlessly and that each of its submodules
    interacts correctly with the others. State verification against
    expected results is also implemented in the automated tests. To
    complement the validation of the numerical results, the software is
    also profiled to ensure its robust performance under continuous
    changes.</p>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p>The software can be used as a standalone simulation tool or as a
  back-end acoustic simulator for the Community Hub for Open-source Room
  Acoustics Software
  (<ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/choras-org/CHORAS">CHORAS</ext-link>)
  (<xref alt="Willemsen et al., 2025" rid="ref-Willemsen2025" ref-type="bibr">Willemsen
  et al., 2025</xref>). The software has already been used in a number
  of scientific publications
  (<xref alt="Fichera et al., 2024" rid="ref-Fichera2024DeterminationApproach" ref-type="bibr">Fichera
  et al., 2024</xref>,
  <xref alt="2025" rid="ref-Fichera2025" ref-type="bibr">2025</xref>,
  <xref alt="2026" rid="ref-Fichera2026AnRooms" ref-type="bibr">2026</xref>)
  and graduate-level teaching at the Eindhoven University of Technology
  (TU/e), where it has proven valuable for understanding the impact of
  room geometry and material absorption on acoustic behavior.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>No generative AI tools were used in the development of this
  software, the writing of this manuscript, or the preparation of
  supporting materials.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="acknowledgements-and-funding">
  <title>Acknowledgements and funding</title>
  <p>The authors acknowledge contributions from Silvin Willemsen, Hassan
  Teymoori, Amin Livani, Felipe Raymann, and Radovan Bast.</p>
  <p>This research is funded by the Dutch Research Council (NWO),
  Applied and Engineering Sciences (AES) under grant agreement
  No. 19430, with the project title “A new era of room acoustics
  simulation software: from academic advances to a sustainable
  open-source project and community”.</p>
</sec>
</body>
